ass "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "56016"} "1896"] "\n" [:option {:value "56065"} "1900"] "\n" [:option {:value "56130"} "1904"] "\n" [:option {:value "56207"} "1906"] "\n" [:option {:value "56349"} "1908"] "\n" [:option {:value "56624"} "1912"] "\n" [:option {:value "57113"} "1920"] "\n" [:option {:value "57390"} "1924"] "\n" [:option {:value "57690"} "1928"] "\n" [:option {:value "57977"} "1932"] "\n" [:option {:value "58217"} "1936"] "\n" [:option {:value "58564"} "1948"] "\n" [:option {:value "58918"} "1952"] "\n" [:option {:value "59317"} "1956"] "\n" [:option {:value "59701"} "1960"] "\n" [:option {:value "60143"} "1964"] "\n" [:option {:value "61086"} "1972"] "\n" [:option {:value "61675"} "1976"] "\n" [:option {:value "62233"} "1980"] "\n" [:option {:value "62710"} "1984"] "\n" [:option {:value "63350"} "1988"] "\n" [:option {:value "64031"} "1992"] "\n" [:option {:value "64710"} "1996"] "\n" [:option {:value "65388"} "2000"] "\n" [:option {:value "66080"} "2004"] "\n" [:option {:value "257212"} "2008"] "\n" [:option {:value "302226"} "2012"] "\n" [:option {:value "358624"} "2016"] "\n" [:option {:value "19000422"} "2020"] "\n" [:option {:value "2013570"} "2024"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/44"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "1,500 metres, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "18 – 20 October 1968"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/479"} "Estadio Olímpico Universitario, Ciudad de México"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/60581"} "54 from 37 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Going into 1968 the heavy favorite was considered to be the American " [:a {:href "/athletes/78987"} "Jim Ryun"] ". Ryun had run at the 1964 Olympics while only a high schooler, but since the Tokyo Olympics, he had broken the mile world record, running 3:51.3 on 17 July 1966 at Berkeley, California. Then at the 1967 AAU Meet, he bettered that with 3:51.1. In July 1967 a dual meet between the USA and the British Commonwealth was planned in Los Angeles. Running for the Commonwealth team was Kenya’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/72762"} "Kip Keino"] ", who was felt to be the best miler in the world, after Ryun. At that event, Ryun and Keino raced over 1,500 metres and Ryun shattered the field and world record with 3:33.1, Keino over four seconds behind. Ryun was felt to be unstoppable."] "\n\n" [:p {} "But the Olympics were in 1968, not 1966 or 1967, and they were held at the altitude of Mexico City. Further, early in 1968, Ryun fell ill with a mild case of mononucleosis which affected his training. He trained in Flagstaff, Arizona to try to acclimatize to altitude but his weakness from the mono made that difficult. Still he made the US Olympic team, and he had been so dominant in 1966-67 that he was considered a slight favorite for the 1,500. Ryun and Keino both passed thru to the final, but another important runner was also there, Keino’s Kenyan teammate, " [:a {:href "/athletes/72756"} "Ben Jipcho"] ". Not considered a medal contender, Jipcho and Keino planned a strategic race trying to weaken Ryun’s deadly kick. Jipcho passed 400 metres in a suicidal 56.0 with Keino on his shoulder in 56.6. Ryun ran in third but was well back and the pace was felt to be far too fast for the 2,200 metre altitude. But Keino pushed on, taking the lead at 800 in 1:55.3. Ryun was 30 metres back. Keino ran his third lap in 58.1, passing 1,200 in 2:53.4, faster than world record pace. Nobody could keep up that pace, it was felt, but Ryun and the field were now so far behind that the race was over. Keino held on to win in an Olympic record 3:34.91, considered unreachable in that thin air. Ryun did win the silver medal, 25 metres back."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "565"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/72762"} "Kip Keino"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/KEN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/KEN.png"}] "KEN"]] [:td {} "3:46.9 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "3:51.4 (2 h2)"] [:td {} "3:34.9 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "300"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/78987"} "Jim Ryun"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "3:45.7 (1 h4)"] [:td {} "3:51.2 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "3:37.8 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "51"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/70552"} "Bodo Tümmler"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRG"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRG.png"}] "FRG"]] [:td {} "3:51.5 (2 h1)"] [:td {} "3:53.6 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "3:39.0 (3)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {:class "bib"} "36"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/70433"} "Harald Norpoth"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRG"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRG.png"}] "FRG"]] [:td {} "3:46.9 (3 h3)"] [:td {} "3:54.3 (5 h1)"] [:td {} "3:42.5 (4)"] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr 